---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// "Linting & migration" — the guide to keeping templates canonical and importing
// Handlebars. Two user-facing tools: `flatbars lint` (warn-only canonicalization,
// for editors and CI) and `flatbars migrate` (Handlebars → MaxBars source, with a
// residual report). Unlike the dialect references this is a CLI/CI guide, not an
// interactive playground — the linter has no Lab view, so the before/after here is
// the exact tool output, gate-validated against the engine (../lint.mjs, gated by
// scripts/check-tutorial-tooling.mjs). The normative text lives in docs/
// (adr-0019-operation-vocabulary, adr-0021-maxbars-variable-model).
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import { lintExamples, migrateExamples, lintLabInput, migrateLabInput } from "../lint.mjs";
import { labHref } from "../../../lab/open-in-lab.mjs";

const t = (s) => s; // readability helper for inline tag literals

// Open-in-Lab parity with the dialect pages: each example carries into the Lab —
// lint examples land with the Lint dock panel open, migrate examples in the
// Migrated MaxBars output view. The deep-links are built at build time from the
// same per-kind target the CI gate validates (lint.mjs). Same env var the
// OpenInLab island uses, so dev and the GitHub-Pages sub-path both resolve.
const LAB_URL = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_LAB_URL || "/lab/index.html";
const lintHref = {};
for (const [k, ex] of Object.entries(lintExamples)) {
  const { engine, example } = lintLabInput(ex);
  lintHref[k] = await labHref(engine, example, { labUrl: LAB_URL });
}
const migrateHref = {};
for (const [k, ex] of Object.entries(migrateExamples)) {
  const { engine, example } = migrateLabInput(ex);
  migrateHref[k] = await labHref(engine, example, { labUrl: LAB_URL });
}

const groups = [
  {
    label: "Linting & migration",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "What the linter does" },
      { href: "#aliases", label: "Deprecated aliases" },
      { href: "#scoped", label: "Scoped variables" },
      { href: "#ci", label: "In the editor & CI" },
      { href: "#migrate", label: "Migrating Handlebars" },
      { href: "#residuals", label: "What migration can't do" },
      { href: "#library", label: "Lower & lift (library)" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Other references",
    items: [
      { href: "/start", label: "Getting started" },
      { href: "/analyse", label: "Truthiness portability" },
      { href: "/maxbars", label: "MaxBars" },
      { href: "/fullbars", label: "FullBars" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=maxbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];
---

<Reference
  title="Linting & migration"
  subtitle="Two terminal tools that keep templates honest: flatbars lint warns on deprecated aliases and non-canonical scoped variables (for your editor and CI), and flatbars migrate rewrites Handlebars source to MaxBars — with a residual report for what it can't safely guess."
  groups={groups}
  dialect="maxbars"
>
  <style>
    .io { display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; margin: 0.9rem 0 1.4rem; }
    .io .lbl { font: 600 11px/1 var(--font-mono); letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--fg-faint); }
    .io pre { margin: 0.15rem 0 0.6rem; }
    .io .arr { color: var(--accent-2); font: 600 12px var(--font-mono); margin: 0.1rem 0; }
    pre.cli { background: var(--bg-0, #11131a); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem; overflow-x: auto; }
    pre.cli code { color: var(--fg-secondary); font: 13px/1.5 var(--font-mono); }
    .warn { color: var(--accent-2); }
    .resid { margin: 0.9rem 0 1.4rem; padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
      background: var(--accent-softer); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; font-size: 0.92rem; }
    .resid b { color: var(--accent-2); }
    .resid .sug { display: block; margin-top: 0.4rem; color: var(--fg-secondary); }
    .oil-link { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.3rem; margin: 0.1rem 0 0.4rem;
      font: 600 12px var(--font-mono); color: var(--accent-2); text-decoration: none;
      border: 1px solid var(--border-strong); border-radius: 6px; padding: 0.25rem 0.6rem;
      background: var(--bg-1); }
    .oil-link:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  </style>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>What the linter does</h2>
    <p>
      The engine accepts more than one spelling for the same thing: a few deprecated
      <b>aliases</b> (<code>plus</code> for <code>add</code>), and legacy spellings of the
      loop <b>scoped variables</b> (<code>index</code> for <code>index0</code>). They render
      identically — so this is never a render error and never blocks output. It is a
      <b>hygiene check</b>: <code>flatbars lint</code> emits one warning per non-canonical
      use, pointing at the canonical name, for your editor to surface and your CI to gate.
    </p>
    <p>
      A separate tool, <a href="#migrate"><code>flatbars migrate</code></a>, is a one-shot
      <b>Handlebars → MaxBars</b> source rewriter for importing existing templates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Aliases ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="aliases">
    <h2>Deprecated aliases</h2>
    <p>
      An alias is a second name the prelude still installs but no longer prefers. The lint
      points at the canonical name; the <a href="#migrate">migrate/lift</a> assist can rewrite
      it for you.
    </p>
    <div class="io">
      <span class="lbl">template</span>
      <pre class="cli"><code>{lintExamples.alias.template}</code></pre>
      <span class="lbl">flatbars lint</span>
      <pre class="cli"><code class="warn">{lintExamples.alias.report}</code></pre>
      <a class="oil-link" href={lintHref.alias}>Open in Lab ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Scoped vars ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="scoped">
    <h2>Scoped variables</h2>
    <p>
      Inside a loop, the canonical names in RawBars/MaxBars are <code>index0</code>,
      <code>rindex0</code>, <code>first</code>, <code>last</code>, <code>key</code>; the bare
      <code>index</code>/<code>rindex</code>/<code>size</code> are legacy spellings. The lint
      steers you to the canonical one.
    </p>
    <div class="io">
      <span class="lbl">template</span>
      <pre class="cli"><code>{lintExamples.scopedVariable.template}</code></pre>
      <span class="lbl">flatbars lint --maxbars</span>
      <pre class="cli"><code class="warn">{lintExamples.scopedVariable.report}</code></pre>
      <a class="oil-link" href={lintHref.scopedVariable}>Open in Lab ↗</a>
    </div>
    <p>
      This lint is <b>surface-scoped</b>: it runs for RawBars/MaxBars, where the bare native
      name is canonical, but <em>not</em> for FullBars — there <code>{t("{{@index}}")}</code>
      is the Handlebars spelling and stays canonical. Run it with <code>--surface</code> and
      only the alias lint applies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────── CI ──────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="ci">
    <h2>In the editor &amp; CI</h2>
    <p>
      <b>In your editor</b> — the FlatBars language server (VS Code, JetBrains) surfaces each
      finding as a diagnostic with a one-click <b>quick-fix</b> that rewrites the name to its
      canonical form. The replacement comes from the same prelude the linter reads, so the
      editor and the CLI can never disagree.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>In CI</b> — a clean template says so, and <code>--max-warnings</code> turns findings
      into a gate (default <code>0</code>: any finding fails the build):
    </p>
    <pre class="cli"><code>$ flatbars lint template.rawbars
{lintExamples.clean.report}

$ flatbars lint src/email.maxbars --maxbars --max-warnings 0   # CI gate</code></pre>
    <a class="oil-link" href={lintHref.clean}>Open a clean template in Lab ↗</a>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Migrate ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="migrate">
    <h2>Migrating Handlebars</h2>
    <p>
      <code>flatbars migrate</code> rewrites Handlebars source to MaxBars, touching only the
      tags that change and slicing the rest from the original — so spacing and
      whitespace-control survive. The mechanical rewrites:
    </p>

    <h3><code>@</code>-data → the <code>loop</code> object</h3>
    <div class="io">
      <pre class="cli"><code>{migrateExamples.loopVars.template}</code></pre>
      <span class="arr">migrate ↓</span>
      <pre class="cli"><code>{migrateExamples.loopVars.source}</code></pre>
      <a class="oil-link" href={migrateHref.loopVars}>Open in Lab ↗</a>
    </div>

    <h3>Inverted section → <code>{t("{{#unless}}")}</code></h3>
    <div class="io">
      <pre class="cli"><code>{migrateExamples.inverted.template}</code></pre>
      <span class="arr">migrate ↓</span>
      <pre class="cli"><code>{migrateExamples.inverted.source}</code></pre>
      <a class="oil-link" href={migrateHref.inverted}>Open in Lab ↗</a>
    </div>

    <h3>Block-partial reference → <code>{t("{{yield}}")}</code></h3>
    <div class="io">
      <pre class="cli"><code>{migrateExamples.partialBlock.template}</code></pre>
      <span class="arr">migrate ↓</span>
      <pre class="cli"><code>{migrateExamples.partialBlock.source}</code></pre>
      <a class="oil-link" href={migrateHref.partialBlock}>Open in Lab ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Residuals ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="residuals">
    <h2>What migration can't do</h2>
    <p>
      Migration is honest about its limits: where a Handlebars construct has no single
      meaning in MaxBars, it leaves the source <em>unchanged</em> and reports a <b>residual</b>
      for a human to resolve — it never guesses.
    </p>
    <div class="io">
      <pre class="cli"><code>{migrateExamples.ambiguousSection.template}</code></pre>
      <span class="arr">migrate ↓ (unchanged)</span>
      <pre class="cli"><code>{migrateExamples.ambiguousSection.source}</code></pre>
      <a class="oil-link" href={migrateHref.ambiguousSection}>Open in Lab ↗</a>
    </div>
    <div class="resid">
      <b>Residual · ambiguous-section.</b> A bare Mustache section <code>{t("{{#widget}}")}</code>
      renders its body when <code>widget</code> is truthy <em>and</em> iterates when it is a
      list — MaxBars splits those into distinct shapes.
      <span class="sug">Choose the intended form: <code>{t("{{#if widget}}…{{/if}}")}</code>
      for a truthy guard, or <code>{t("{{#each widget}}…{{/each}}")}</code> to iterate.</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      The other residual is <b>set-delimiters</b> (<code>{t("{{=<% %>=}}")}</code>): MaxBars
      has no delimiter switching, so it is reported, not rewritten.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Lower & lift ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="library">
    <h2>Lower &amp; lift (library)</h2>
    <p>
      Two more transforms exist as <b>library functions</b>, not CLI commands, because they
      are round-trip plumbing rather than everyday tasks: <code>lower</code> prints a MaxBars
      template as the equivalent austere <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> core source, and
      <code>lift</code> heuristically re-sugars core source back to MaxBars (flagging the
      ambiguous spots). They are the proof that the surface desugaring is reversible; reach
      for them from the <code>linter</code> package, not the terminal.
    </p>
  </section>
</Reference>
